<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry of Laach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1095</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry of Laach was a German nobleman who is the first person recorded with the title count palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, around 1085 to 1087. Earlier counts palatine of Lotharingia had served as royal deputies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, and Henry moved the office's centre to the middle Rhine. With his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-countess-of-vermandois">Adelaide</a> he founded the Benedictine abbey of Maria Laach in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eifel">Eifel</a> in 1093. His office developed into the Electoral Palatinate.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-of-laach.jpg" alt="Henry of Laach" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Weinandt · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grabmal_Heinrich_II_von_Laach_Abtei_Maria_Laach_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
